<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Wave C ~ the hearth-stone carries the name

Wave C builds `K15` step 6, the fence, under one identity model the operator approved on 2026-10-20: //sync carries; verification decides// (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road`>> #/rulings; the research at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026`>>). Beneath it stands the moʻolelo's first line: the truth rests in the minds of the operators, and the CRDTs carry it to the synced stores. Wave C launches once Wave B lands on a green span; `W26`'s slices 3, 4 and 6 run beside it on the floor's own ground; its slice 5 follows, since the accepted floor rests as a face's D row.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-stories`>>

!! The stories

''Kalani's laptop forgets nothing it swore to.'' Someone with write access to Kalani's synced docs rewrites the daemon doc to name another face's group and KEL prefix, with a perfectly valid edge from that other face. Kalani's laptop boots, reads its own sealed anchor, sees a bundle it never swore to, and refuses by name. The crier shouted a lie; the stone kept the name.

''The Council's lease never runs backward.'' A stale replica of the Council's daemon doc arrives missing the newest lease slot. Before Wave C, an old device edge reads current against it. After it, each device keeps a sealed frontier that never falls, and the stale replica moves nothing.

''A tab knows its face before it speaks.'' Kalani opens a tab; it reads the face's pins from its custody anchor at unlock, never from a document, then proves itself to the holder. The tab's own Repo retires; each face's house Repo serves its documents.

''Two faces, two shelves of books.'' Kalani's browser wears their own face and the Council's, each with its own social planes keyed by its PersonaGroup, as the node already holds them.

''The myth.'' The village crier runs fast and everywhere, and wrong whenever the last shout lied. A family's true name stays carved in its own hearth-stone, where only founding, admission or recovery may carve. The crier repeats the stone; no shout changes it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-reds`>>

!! The reds ~ grounds before wins, each written as the specification, all proven at the wave's end

# ''The sealed anchor.'' Each face holds, on each device, a D row: its PersonaGroup, persona-KEL prefix, a commitment to its root, its edge's digest, and a known frontier that never falls. Founding, admission and the recovery rites write it; sync never does. Red: a forged selector row in the daemon doc, then a coherent valid foreign bundle, switch no pinned face; a missing anchor refuses face boot by name.
# ''The signed `@persona` log.'' Founding and admission acts, device edges and lease advances travel as signed entries in the face's `@persona` plane, verified against the anchor and the KEL; the reader folds them as the KEL board does. Red: two signed acts at one sequence surface as a fork, never a CRDT winner.
# ''The monotone frontier.'' Lease advances carry signatures in the per-writer shape (`lease-advance`: each writer signs `{resource, writer, epoch}` into its own slot); the sealed local frontier never falls. A lease advance rides a per-writer signed slot and never the `@persona` log, since two writers advancing at once would fork a hash-linked log, and the house names forks and never resolves them; per-writer slots let concurrent writers both land while the max climbs. A face's advance is `lares persona lease`, and the boot raises the anchor only from slots that verify (`lease-observation`). Red: a deleted, replayed or unsigned high slot lowers nothing and raises nothing. An act that widens authority (admit, grant, re-key, seal to a reader) refuses a target naming a newer bound than the frontier holds, its receipt naming the frontier it acted under.
# ''Verified projections.'' The face planes and the daemon doc's identity tiddlers read as projections of verified records, disposable and rebuilt. Red: a plane URL with no signed founding or admission act behind it never registers.
# ''The FCK fence.'' Each face's D rows (atom, archive, anchor, custody root, proof keys) seal under that face's own random FCK and key by its PersonaGroup on both hosts; the vessel key opens none of them. Red: K15's isolation red at the key-class level, where Wave B's `test.fails` rows turn green: unlock A alone, and every key A holds, the vessel key included, opens no B row.
# ''The daemon's last unions'' (Wave B's census, node items 4 to 6): one keyhive, face ledger, content-key seat and seat-roll set per face, never one per daemon; face-join and seat duties reseal the asking face's bags alone; CLI selectors name their face and never default to `h0`; `fleet-join-pending` keys by face; a joined face's hearth dial pin stands per face.
# ''One SharedWorker per house.'' A tab knows its face from its custody anchor before it connects, then opens the SharedWorker whose `name` digests that house's (PersonaGroup, persona root); the house's island runs in that worker's own thread and realm, with no nested Worker (Chromium exposes none inside a SharedWorker), so each face keeps its own realm, one island per house, and a house outlives any one of its tabs. Red, in a real browser on every engine: two faces' tabs reach two worker instances, a second tab of one face reaches the same one, and the last tab's close ends the house.
# ''The house holder answers, never stalls'' (Wave B's seventh fresh read, carried). A floor or house worker that never announces `ready` refuses its held asks by name and is never left waiting; a replay posts its replacement manifest before it retires the crossing it replaces; a missing seat-roll ledger keeps one pending find per document, never one per gate read; the C4 boot trace's manifest marker fires on the real source, proven by a control that runs `instrumentBootSource` over the daemon and counts one `host:manifest-post`; the pre-clear `doc:rested` carries a storage proof from the house's own rest shore. Red: each of these, every one through an event or causal read, never a timer that decides.
# ''The tab reads its pins from custody'' and the tab's own Repo retires; ''each face holds its own social planes in the browser'', keyed by PersonaGroup.
# ''The place card.'' Every node joinee holds a place card from its own place founding; a joinee path that reaches a place-arm presentation without one reds, cured at the place founding. A joinee whose place slot holds the FACE's card (or the founder's) refuses by name, and the cure never writes a face's card into the place slot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/beside-it`>>

!! Beside it ~ the edge frontier

Row `K17`, the signed edge frontier (#/rulings, the edge frontier), shares Wave C's signed-log pattern and builds beside it: the frontier certificate, the genesis frontier at every founding, raise registration, the widening reader, and the admit bundle carrying the frontier. Wave C's anchor and `K17`'s frontier read one verifier style on node and browser.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-crew`>>

!! The crew ~ very large work chunks under the mixed-model-swarm protocols

One joint lane (`feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--k15-wave-c`), one pull request, landed whole and merged by train. A Sonnet Episkopos leads; the Codex Sol (`gpt-6-sol`, walled) builds; the Copilot seat (`gpt-5.3-codex`) reads, scouts and sweeps (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>). The battery rides CI's shards: locally the wave runs its build and each red's proof against the base tree, pushes once, and reads lane CI, held-out and the shards. A fresh Sol reader, from a lineage that built none of it, reads the whole diff before the train. The scout census opens the lane: every place an identity pin reads from a mutable doc without verification, by file and line, sorted by what inherits shape from what.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>

!! Owed beyond Wave C

* ''The recovery door: recovery-contact-quorum rotation, then `reanchorAfterRotation`, then a re-admit carrying the log and frontier.'' No production door rotates a persona root beneath its KEL prefix: `reanchorAfterRotation` stands proven as a library function with its red and control (a face recovered to a new root boots against the rewritten anchor; a document naming the new root with no KEL rotation behind it refuses), and `runReadmitEdge` has no production caller. Building the door means the recovery-contact-quorum ceremony, whose recovery contact count is an open operator gate (`kahu#/seed-and-nexus`). The re-admit then carries the current `@persona` log and the signed frontier as a first admit does.
* ''The FCK fence'' leaves this wave whole and becomes Wave D (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-d-lane`>>). The two key-class `test.fails` rows in `face-isolation-routes.test.ts` stand for it.
* ''A face's circles and identities follow the face'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-22): minted once at founding, named by a signed `plane` act, carried in the admit bundle, adopted by every admitted device, and named in the face proof beside the persona plane (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-circle`>> #/the-atom). Sessions stay per vessel, as motion.
* ''A device's own entry joins the face's shared documents when they ARRIVE'': a fresh device holds neither document at its admit, so `joinFaceDocumentsOnArrival` subscribes to the replica's own progress view and writes its identity entry and ceremony circles the moment the document is ready, once. No reboot and no timer decide it, and a vessel outside the group never reaches it. Red and control: `join-face-documents.test.ts`.
* ''The retired-key window after a lost-and-recovered offline device'': an act beyond a reader's accepted frontier signs under the KEL's current epoch, and a lease advance counts only from the current roster. A legitimate act or advance signed under the old epoch that this reader had not yet accepted therefore needs re-signing under the new epoch; the recovery door (K18) owns that re-sign.
* ''A browser holding several faces'' takes the dedicated-worker floor (`houseOfBoot` reads the founding bootstrap and refuses when more than one persona root stands), and its worn second face proves its persona plane alone: the founding bootstrap names one face's circles and identities. Both ride Wave D's browser mirror (slice 4), where each face keeps its own documents and key.
* ''A joinee that boots from its daemon-carried act bundle'' keeps that projection until a reboot when a later persona-plane replica carries a competing same-sequence act. The arrival-triggered re-proof and what it does on a fork belong to K18, beside the recovery door and the equivocation handling.
* ''End-to-end gate controls'': the faced-boot composer controls read source order and the admit door's face-edge suite mocks the log projection and the lease read; a sealed-vessel run that inspects catalog side effects, and the real-engine holder lifecycle in a browser whose module graph greets (the Chromium and Firefox harness here floors, so those cases skip), ride the container weld in `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh`.
* ''The holder's teardown backstop'' keeps one five-second timer that completes a teardown whose island never acknowledged. It decides nothing about identity, a frontier or a greeting; the house is already ended when it fires.
* ''The holder door takes the user's cancel'': `attachSharedHolder` sets no deadline, so a worker that neither greets nor errors waits forever. Every production caller passes a `signal` tied to the user's intent (the tab closing, a face switch, a user abort). `open-browser-vessel.ts` holds no such signal today (`BrowserVesselOptions` carries none), so the wiring and its red (a silent worker plus a user abort floors the boot at once) ride the first lane that gives the vessel open an abort path.
* ''K15-H: the Handle owner rides the anchor'' (a new lane, scheduled after Wave C beside Wave D where the file sets prove disjoint; K18 owns the recovery door, not this). Scope: Handle founding, burn and rotation take the owner prefix from the sealed anchor through `readFace`, and `headOpKey` goes behind the door on both shores. The census (`face-reading-census.test.ts`) names the files by spelling until they migrate: `commands/handle.ts`, `browser-handle-verbs.ts` and `handle-publish.ts`. Read 4's two findings, verbatim. FINDING 1: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-mesh/src/handle-publish.ts:25-46`; `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:47-79`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-publish.ts:23-40` — Handle founding takes its owner Persona KEL prefix straight from a mutable daemon tiddler, outside `readFace`. The seed is loaded by selected handle index, but `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc` never checks the selected face's sealed anchor, signed founding act, or verified KEL lineage. A daemon-document change can choose a foreign or stale prefix for the signed public Handle inception. The downstream Handle chain treats the owner prefix as part of its own signed inception, so the local signer can permanently publish the wrong owner binding even when the face boot would reject that selector. The census contains none of `publishHandleFromDaemonDoc`, `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER`, or `publishPersonaGlamour` in its primitive or decision-site lists. FINDING 2: MEASURED — `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/handle.ts:224-235,269-281`; `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-handle-verbs.ts:123-141` — owner burn and Handle rotation resolve owner authority from the mutable daemon prefix through a separate `headOpKey` verifier, outside `readFace`. These commands take `PERSONA_KEL_PREFIX_TIDDLER` from the daemon doc, resolve its board chain, and accept a matching root seed as the authorizing hand. The check proves that *some* chain has that current key; it does not prove that this Handle's owner member prefix is the selected face's sealed anchor prefix or the Handle inception's owner set. The Handle KEL's own membership verifier may still reject a mismatched owner prefix; the direct authority decision at the local command is nonetheless outside the door, and the census misses the entire `headOpKey` spelling. The same raw-prefix pattern serves both Node and browser.
* ''The fleet frontier's pin lives in the vessel's own mutable ledger'' (read 4's LEAD against `edge-frontier-board.ts` and `edge-kapae.ts`): a vessel that loses that ledger and then reads a stale board under an older valid frontier reads an absent edge as clear. A reader with a pin refuses a board that does not extend it (`does-not-extend-pin`, red in `edge-frontier.test.ts`), and a vessel with no pin at all is a fresh one, which cannot tell a stale board from a young fleet. Pinning the fleet head in the sealed anchor, beside the act head and the lease, belongs to K17.
* ''Read 5's remaining MEDIUMs'' (5 HIGH cured in the lane, MEDIUMs named, none authority-bearing past the proof): the admit preflight checks that a frontier record exists but leaves its signed hand, group, roster and snapshot to the later boot's reader (`ceremony-core.ts`); the issuer stamps the signed admission's epoch from the raw chain head, where the verified lineage's last epoch would match (`device-admit.ts`); the fleet-join offer reads its group through `facePersonaGroup` from the bootstrap on the founding face (`fleet-join.ts`) and `init.ts` reads the bootstrap group to choose `alreadyStood`; a holder session whose lock or proof rejects after the greeting leaves queued asks waiting without a named refusal (`shared-holder.ts`); the browser's second face neither joins nor proves its identities and circles documents (Wave D slice 4). Each rides its owning lane's row: K17 for the frontier verification, K15-H for the Handle owner, Wave D for the browser faces.
* ''Read 6's two lane-owned HIGH, written verbatim as Wave D's opening rows'' (read 6 found no P0: an unaffiliated wire peer reached no face act and no private plane). ROW 1: MEASURED — lane-owned composition, foreign face share falls open when its catalog row is absent. `packages/lararium-node/src/face-fleet-view.ts:76-87,131-137`; `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:753-754`. One-line reason: the worn face bridge allows *every* document id outside a negative set derived from the mutable catalog, so deleting or withholding a foreign face's row before a new session is seated removes that face's private plane from the deny set. This is the exact inverse of the proposed authority direction: an unverified catalog *absence* changes a share from false to true. The `bb8513247` repair added `foreignFaceDocs`, but the live verdict is `view.fetch(id).ok || !others.has(id)`; a missing catalog entry makes `others.has` false. The source test itself demonstrates the relevant behavior at `face-fleet-view.test.ts:122-140`: with only the worn bootstrap, the bare bridge reads the other face's circles, and supplying a catalog-derived `foreign` set blocks it. The control never tries the catalog row deleted after registration, or a held other-face document whose catalog entry was omitted. A same-operator session of the worn face can fetch the other face's held document by id in that state. The verdict is wired through `shareConfigOf` to both the announce and access hooks (`federation-gate.ts:602-611`), so this is an access decision rather than an announcement-only cosmetic leak; the live transport control at `share-policy-is-access.test.ts:49-67` demonstrates that distinction. This is not P0 under the user's definition because the reader already has an admitted face; the leak crosses face compartments on one vessel. The authorization decision must come from the sealed/proven face-document inventory or a positive view, rather than catalog completeness. The broad bridge predates Wave C (Wave B/O1), but Wave C owns the `foreignFaceDocs` repair and its composer wiring; this finding concerns that lane-owned repair, not the inherited bridge in isolation. ROW 2: MEASURED — lane-owned node daemon keeps a lease floor older than the sealed anchor it raises at boot. `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts:2260-2271,2311-2346`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts:306-321,421-423,704-743,986-1008`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/lease-frontier.ts:64-70`. One-line reason: the composer snapshots `standingAnchor` into `daemonAuth` and awaits `openDaemonVm` *before* it raises and persists the verified lease epoch; the running daemon's live join and grant reads keep flooring under the old object. For a concrete counterexample, start with sealed lease epoch 0 and a verified signed advance to 7 in the mirrored daemon doc. The daemon receives anchor 0, starts, then the parent persists anchor 7 at `:2346`. If that mutable slot is deleted or replayed to 0 during the same daemon lifetime, `readLeaseUnderFace` reads 0 and floors against the daemon's captured anchor 0, despite custody now pinning 7. Its live face-join at `operator-daemon-behavior.ts:704-743` passes the lowered `lease.n` into a new seat and re-seal, while grant ingest at `:306-321` verifies an older bound. A restart loads 7 and closes the window; the claim that the sealed frontier never falls in a standing daemon does not hold. This is Wave C's lease-observation and composer ordering, not an inherited timer. The seam should raise the anchor before forming/sending `daemonAuth`, or deliver the raised pin to the daemon through a verified update. The existing red at `signed-lease-frontier.test.ts:50-61` hands `readLeaseUnderFace` an anchor already at 7, and `lease-observation.test.ts:26` tests the pure raise, so both can pass while the composer sends 0 before it persists 7. A red needs the live daemon, a signed advance, a subsequent slot deletion, and a control that the older edge or grant remains refused without restart.
* ''The realm leg'' is its own lane, K17-R (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k17-realm-lease-lane`>>): one lease law on the realm boards, landed after Wave C.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/edges`>>

!! Edges ~ held outside Wave C

* ''Transparency and witnesses'' for equivocation across devices: their own row, later.
* ''The far agent's relay seat'': its research after `I1`.
* ''The signer frames each floor duty's domain'': `X0`.
* ''Unsigned `requestedBy` on synced summonses'': `I1` moves summonses onto `@intents`, where each entry carries its own signature.

<<~/ahu>>

